Here is the Java program to print all permutations of an array:

```java
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {1, 2, 3};
        permute(arr, 0, arr.length - 1);
    }

    public static void permute(int[] arr, int l, int r) {
        if (l == r)
            System.out.println(Arrays.toString(arr));
        else {
            for (int i = l; i <= r; i++) {
                arr = swap(arr, l, i);
                permute(arr, l + 1, r);
                arr = swap(arr, l, i);
            }
        }
    }

    public static int[] swap(int[] arr, int i, int j) {
        int t = arr[i];
        arr[i] = arr[j];
        arr[j] = t;
        return arr;
    }
}
```

This program uses recursion to find all permutations of the input array. The `permute` function is a recursive function that takes an array and two indices (start and end) as input. It prints the permutation when the start index is equal to the end index. Otherwise, it iterates over the array from the start index to the end index, swaps each element with the start element, recursively calls `permute` with the start index incremented by 1, and then swaps the elements back.

